  "Step 4 · glass type and panes",
  "Step 4 · Pricing estimator",
  "Step 4 of a quotation establishes which sheet a group is cut from and what air space the unit has. Step 5 establishes the W1/H1/W2/H2 grid. If the job order receives only a total area and a price, the factory is working from a summary of a decision it cannot see. The grid geometry is what should cross.",
  "Step 5 · Specification",
  "Step 5 and Step 9, working.",
  "Step 5 holds the specification: # · GLASS REF · W1 · H1 · W2 · H2 · QTY · SQM · TOTAL SQM · RATE · VALUE, on a block carrying GLASS TYPE · SPECIFICATION · BASE · SURCHARGE %. A dash in W2 or H2 is a single-glazed line — the application stores 0 in those cells, as it does in the seeded job on the worked example. Two rules do the pricing and both are live here: every panel bills at least 0.5 m² (the rate is unchanged — the floor is on the area), and a panel that reaches 4 m² takes the specification's surcharge rate instead of its base rate. Step 9 then costs the same rows at the Sheet Store's purchase price and answers the only question that matters: is the given price above target?",
  "Step 5 re-prices a row the moment its billable area reaches {v0} m²: `Sqm1 >= {v1} ? SurchargeRate : BaseRate`. Oversize lites are the ones that break, that need two people and a crane, and that cut badly from a standard sheet, so the specification carries a SURCHARGE % beside its base rate and {v2}% is the shop's standard for it. Only the rows that reach the size change — the rest of the specification stays at base — and if somebody types a rate over by hand the application leaves it alone.",
  "Step 5 specification grid",
  "Step 6 charges that raise floor work",
  "Step 6 rows",
  "Step 9 · Profit & loss",
  "Step 9 asks one question",
  "Step 9 costs these same panels at the purchase price.",
  "Step 9 is the application's only profit-and-loss step, and it asks one question: is the given price above target? Revenue is what the specification bills; the glass cost is the same billable square metres at the Sheet Store purchase price; the target is the base price plus the expected margin. Every row below is a seeded stock line, and the sell column is the rate the quotation above charges — which is why the two figures reconcile rather than merely agreeing.",
  "Step 9 is the only place money is computed in the whole application, and it answers one question — is the given price above target? Everything downstream — job profitability, statements, reports — reads the snapshot it writes.",
  "Step 9 profit & loss: overview, services, sheets and a spec-by-spec check",
  "Step 9 profit & loss: overview, services, sheets and a spec-by-spec check — the single pricing engine, whose snapshot everything downstream reads",
  "Step 9 profit and loss",
  "Step 9 serves as the authoritative pricing engine. The calculated price basis and profit snapshot are stamped permanently onto the quotation record to prevent retrospective margin drift.",
