  "The roadmap, honestly",
  "The rule this page keeps",
  "The rule this page keeps: a partner is named here only after something is agreed in writing. Until then this page states what is being asked for, and nothing more.",
  "The run records a weak lower bound on the sheet count (area only) and reports the optimality gap — so 'how close was this?' has an answer instead of a claim.",
  "The run the result belongs to.",
  "The sales representative is carried on the inquiry and the proforma, so performance is measured from the documents themselves rather than from a separate tally. Value, conversion and profitability sit on the same row.",
  "The same accent wash every card on the site carries.",
  "The same data, six times",
  "The same day is happening in glass shops everywhere",
  "The same flow, seen as one board from Cutting to Dispatch-ready.",
  "The same four-step generator pattern as the order report, over the customer's invoices: what was issued, what is outstanding, what is overdue and an ageing analysis — with an optional combined PDF that merges the signed documents into the same file.",
  "The same glass gets typed five times, and the fifth number never matches the first.",
  "The same glass, typed again",
  "The same habit sets the pace. Twenty-three findings with eleven still open, one phase in progress and the rest not started, is what thoroughness looks like from the outside — and it is the honest reason this page exists rather than a finished product announcement.",
  "The same inputs produce the same number every time, with no hidden state. If two screens show a figure, both came from the same computation.",
  "The same job, worked two ways.",
  "The same receivables picture, grouped by the issuing company instead.",
  "The same rule the sponsorship page keeps: help funds work. It does not buy a claim on this website that the software cannot demonstrate.",
  "The same submission figures as the finance module, presented within the reporting rail so a period close does not mean switching tools twice.",
  "The second pass answered, to file and line, which object holds the cutting sizes when a job is selected, which collections the SIZES tab binds to, and where the values disappear between the two. The answer was four conversion boundaries: a Step 5 filter that checked width but not height; a stock lookup that returned false on a cold cache, so the first run of the day had no racks at all; DGU and lamination builds that carry no sheet sizes by design; and a race in which a view-model's constructor loaded jobs on a background thread and, finishing late, cleared the cutting list that had just been built.",
  "The seeded job is a demonstration. Yours would be your own.",
  "The sequence is the order the packer placed the pieces, which is the order the schedule lists and the operator works through. The application's table carries a seventh column, the glass specification the run was composed from; this sample job is one thickness, so it is stated in the panel header instead.",
  "The sheet is not entirely usable",
  "The sheet price used at the moment of quoting is part of the quotation. When stock prices move next month, that quote should still show the margin it was built on, and the movement should be flagged rather than silently applied.",
  "The sheet store and the consumable inventory are seeded alongside the job, because a glass business is not only glass. Available quantity is stock minus used — the application's own live fields, not a derived figure.",
  "The sheet store keeps buy and use movements per sheet, so offcuts and remnants are stock the system knows about, and a sheet's history stays attached to it.",
  "The sheet-by-sheet schedule with its cuts in execution order.",
  "The single most important property of an ERP is that a number, once stored, stays what it was. ProGlass writes the calculation context next to the figure and reads it back.",
  "the site's own spacing",
  "The sixteen accents the application ships. Tap one and the whole site takes it.",
  "The sizes this sample job is cut from",
  "The software began as a way to stop retyping. It was not planned as a product, and there was no intention to sell it. It was one estimator removing the parts of his own day he kept getting wrong — and then discovering that the same day is happening in glass shops everywhere.",
  "The software is one person.",
  "The source is named wherever the number appears — yours, as published.",
  "The specification grid on step 5: # · glass ref · W1 H1 W2 H2 · qty · SQM · total SQM · rate · value, on a block carrying glass type, specification, base rate and SURCHARGE %",
  "The specification grid the quotation is priced from, and the profit-and-loss step that asks whether the price is good enough. Everything below is live: the grid prices as you type, and the profit-and-loss screen reads those same rows.",
  "The state",
  "The state of the build as it is — the open findings included — and a direct line to the person writing it.",
  "the Step 5 billable area costed at purchase — {v0}% of the sheet spend",
  "The stock behind it",
  "The strategy family decides which placement portfolios get tried. Effort decides how hard the engine searches inside that family — time budget, shuffles and consolidation sweeps. Changing one does not silently change the other.",
  "The synced PI summary does not include pane treatment or complete Step 6 charge details. Review tempering, holes, polish, edge work and mitring before release; add any missing stages and quantities below.",
  "The ten consumable items seeded into inventory.",
  "The terms in advance",
  "The thermal stage — and the routing rule that decides which panes ever reach it.",
  "The thick bucket is deliberately asymmetric: thick glass takes no trim on the top edge. The minimum breakout is the narrowest offcut worth keeping — anything thinner is reported as trim waste rather than counted as reusable stock.",
  "the Thin profile",
  "The thing to ask any optimizer for",
  "The topic list is deliberately narrow. It follows the questions a glass business actually has to answer, not the keywords a software website usually chases.",
  "The total stock figure tells you how much glass you own. Consumption grouped by job tells you where it went, and that is the number that lets you price the next similar job with confidence.",
  "The trade came first",
  "The trade continues inside the guillotine family. Two-stage patterns restrict the cut direction: first all the cuts one way, then all the cuts the other. They are fast, easy to set up and easy to jig, and they leave a bit more waste. Free guillotine allows unlimited depth and packs tighter, at the cost of more table time. Three-stage sits in between. Which one is right depends on your table, your volume and the job.",
  "the treatment is not counted inside the given price on this specification",
  "The trial",
  "The trial is issued automatically the first time the application runs.",
  "The trims and the blade gap are the application's own house defaults, resolved from the glass thickness; the setting itself is the thickness. Everything below it is printed as the value it holds in this preview rather than offered as a control that would not change anything.",
  "The twenty-two sizes this sample job is cut from",
  "The vocabulary of a glass order",
  "The website does not store form submissions in a database. When a mail transport is configured the request is relayed to the developer's inbox; when none is configured the form says so plainly and asks you to email instead, rather than pretending the message was delivered.",
  "The website's own palette — the application's Violet accent, taken deep.",
  "The whole application, grouped as its own rail groups it.",
  "The widest shuffle set and MaxRects on every ordering. Expect minutes on a large job, not seconds — the app says so in its own hint text.",
  "The working day the software was written to fix — see the estimator's day below.",
  "The years already in it, not wasted",
